 "antisymmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417417} {:answer/id #uuid "79527f35-52e1-5824-aacb-f7f05d663924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not antisymmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79527f35-52e1-5824-aacb-f7f05d663924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not antisymmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417454} :answer-field/id #uuid "866fa40e-f8d8-5cbe-8724-d6056076907b" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417576} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b5f8f60-0d74-502f-b467-1aea80eb05bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417477} {:answer/id #uuid "d6af8410-eb76-547a-996a-a74c4d5fa487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b5f8f60-0d74-502f-b467-1aea80eb05bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417477} :answer-field/id #uuid "c65e2230-e4b5-5996-a967-71c371a504a0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417586} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fa25fe6-9664-5441-b6d3-92c0a4761226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)\\notin R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417460} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2aa31f-0d42-56e7-b379-5ad7a318921f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)\\notin R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae2aa31f-0d42-56e7-b379-5ad7a318921f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)\\notin R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417487} :answer-field/id #uuid "d284652a-bb13-5c6a-8a1d-ac6cfcb5ff46" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a49a62b6-c1a4-59cc-8f5e-97f30c5aabcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-213298" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={0,1,2}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(0,0),(0,1),(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}\nand\n{{field-2}}, \nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}.\n\nSince \n{{field-4}}\nand\n{{field-5}}, \nthe relation is \n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nOur relation is defined on ${0,1,2}.$ Notice that:\n\n- $(0,1)\\in R$ and $(1,0)\\notin R.$ So, the relation is $\\text{not symmetric}.$\n\n- $(1,2)\\in R$ and $(2,1)\\in R.$ So, the relation is $\\text{not antisymmetric}.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the relation is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202044b9-b12b-566f-9106-e35394fe4818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417395} {:answer/id #uuid "2a78b387-8979-566f-83e2-812eb5fdf2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417405} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0851ee-3280-534b-892e-859a3e15955f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417432} {:answer/id #uuid "aeff68f4-b03f-5d82-baed-3043b8eda361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417489} {:answer/id #uuid "c70c4806-8925-5b32-88f9-652a7f9a3a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a78b387-8979-566f-83e2-812eb5fdf2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417405} :answer-field/id #uuid "c99522de-cf4d-5f54-a8c5-8dd127c69656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e6d9019-7f0e-55d6-8ba5-3c61199bf5af" :question/math-academy-id "q-213311" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdbcfe164fcca0e005255de10f10c10bedc156f3a582eeb5800322d4c4ebe0ab.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent symmetric relations defined on the set $A={0,2,4}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/9c/9c9c16300cfb95a61a261e39db865d5c6d056794bd7aaa422567bde10dd4abf5.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(4,2)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(2,4)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(0,4)$ and $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c5373d-0629-56d7-901e-7af879dd9ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417386} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc38fe7-4cc0-5b89-838a-99b8f54f414a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417392} {:answer/id #uuid "32e8c8fd-be74-587d-a3b4-8214f956aaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417412} {:answer/id #uuid "8281f135-368b-5bd3-8ea5-ebad2352c486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417463} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7470ff-d87b-54d1-8fb8-68a8dbdf78fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc38fe7-4cc0-5b89-838a-99b8f54f414a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417392} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a672eb5-09d4-52a3-990d-6a002fb2c6dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84454995-d49e-5edd-80f6-dfbaab2558d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-213318" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8ebf81e13d905d36997abf2f16c3732714b4feb56334f5e0c56443aa07732c6.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents an antisymmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $3\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 3$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f60a9114-56fc-5360-ae43-4601b4dee096" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From Adjacency Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1366d776-b1cf-5d05-ba47-1198ddc13169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{?}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417388} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebc7295-62c6-5e01-8f31-506b5fcd4f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417433} {:answer/id #uuid "ef78d06e-e5ab-5f31-956d-6f8b331e1192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1366d776-b1cf-5d05-ba47-1198ddc13169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{?}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417388} :answer-field/id #uuid "528cc93b-7bc5-5c38-9cfa-8fc725eaffe3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860