r-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02f7c6b3-d7d6-5678-a0b7-ed30b642b1bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-213371" :question/problem "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A={1,2,3}.$ Given that $R$ is antisymmetric, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix. \n\n**Note**: If there is not enough information to determine a particular cell's value, then select the question mark $(\\text{?}).$\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWe are given elements of the adjacency matrix in the following highlighted cells outside the diagonal:\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |\n| $3$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAs a result, the relation contains the pairs $(1,3)$ and $(3,2).$ Since $R$ must be antisymmetric, we require \n\n$$\n(3,1)\\notin R,\\,(2,3)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nSo, the cells corresponding to these elements must all be $0.$\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nWe do not have enough information to determine whether $(1,2)$ is an element of $R.$ \n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $?$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f60a9114-56fc-5360-ae43-4601b4dee096" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From Adjacency Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73f92e4c-2327-5d94-94b1-6b3912171a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417449} {:answer/id #uuid "a123e37e-1a98-5dd4-9865-f32a26b3c2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417480} {:answer/id #uuid "ef626fef-22f6-5946-a9ca-ea0ac454c2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{?}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a123e37e-1a98-5dd4-9865-f32a26b3c2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417480} :answer-field/id #uuid "28b1826a-f40a-5dda-9e62-aca68804c4c1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417556} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f9755b9-2947-5bd0-b1ee-83c94d44afdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{?}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417434} {:answer/id #uuid "a09c21c9-a1e1-59da-ab83-be5325924d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417479} {:answer/id #uuid "a32e0134-f213-522f-bbe0-29cc1c64eb26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #e