d "fc83300e-7a59-523f-b571-35264613ed6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc83300e-7a59-523f-b571-35264613ed6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417544} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7950904-3c40-596d-ac53-ce1c8b5f7021" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be0263b8-da6c-5757-b44e-2150685ff381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417498} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a43c50-a4d7-589a-a714-f4ff32df2442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be0263b8-da6c-5757-b44e-2150685ff381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417498} :answer-field/id #uuid "efda5b75-970c-5d64-8c59-14946d984faa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417598} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e823f2-bcb9-5505-9e22-c4765433fa1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417382} {:answer/id #uuid "a8246496-c116-50a2-a32a-c2e72bf37892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8246496-c116-50a2-a32a-c2e72bf37892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417483} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6de0020-8ee8-5ff1-8ab7-98a147556959" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b24ff238-78b5-5737-b5c9-c38496b2d157" :question/math-academy-id "q-213487" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={-1,0,2}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(-1,2),(2,-1),(0,0),(0,2)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}\nand\n{{field-2}}, \nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}.\n\nSince \n{{field-4}}\nand\n{{field-5}}, \nthe relation is \n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nOur relation is defined on ${0,1,2}.$ Notice that:\n\n- $(-1,2)\\in R$ and $(2,-1)\\in R.$ So, the relation is $\\text{not antisymmetric}.$\n\n- $(0,2)\\in R$ and $(2,0)\\notin R.$ So, the relation is $\\text{not symmetric}.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the relation is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d90713-bab4-5982-9789-c8022a2a41da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417380} {:answer/id #uuid "20bd4423-75c9-5c3e-b7e3-27c624187a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417396} {:answer/id #uuid "353df1a9-635f-5637-a998-3ec12de5fb5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417414} {:answer/id #uuid "563b2cb6-9661-5e33-b3af-530622715f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417430} {:answer/id #uuid "96510da6-be83-5df5-9b88-5179df3edb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "353df1a9-635f-5637-a998-3ec12de5fb5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417414} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bc889b-d302-50fa-aa28-ad159b53a97d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92e553e5-9efc-54d5-9f9a-59a998ba0a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-213492" :question/problem "![](images/88/88e4ad0704f54f33241180593dc1ce4fe384710d5a680717f7c0449d5f85ec2c.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represents a relation that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/cd/cd4c4ac0b0b4c00e8cb29e8c95738d5c9c4404113c5370791e2277ca66cdbda2.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(a,b)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(b,a)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(a,c)$ and $(c,a).$\n\n- Plot III represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f60a9114-56fc-5360-ae43-4601b4dee096" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From Adjacency Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0439273-6263-5aa1-a002-b3811af18abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417501} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a82df7-a027-5a4e-8fdd-818137f8e88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{?}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417525} {:answer/