d/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdbdaa55-4775-5c9f-83c4-cb845bb96951" :question/math-academy-id "q-213497" :question/problem "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A={-4,2,8}.$ Given that $R$ is symmetric, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix. \n\n**Note**: If there is not enough information to determine a particular cell's value, then select the question mark $(\\text{?}).$\n\n|  | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| $8$ | {{field-4}} | $1$ | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWe are given elements of the adjacency matrix in the following highlighted cells outside the diagonal:  \n\n|  | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $0$ |  | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |  |  |\n| $8$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAs a result, the relation contains the pair $(8,2).$ Since $R$ must be symmetric, we require \n\n$$\n(2,8)\\in R.\n$$\n\nSo, the cell corresponding to this element must be equal to $1.$\n\n|  | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $0$ |  | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |  | $1$ |\n| $8$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAlso, note the following:\n\n- The symmetric relation condition is equivalent to $(x,y)\\notin R⟹(y,x)\\notin R.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n(-4,8)\\notin R⟹(8,-4)\\notin R,\\,\\text{and}\\,(2,-4)\\notin R⟹(-4,2)\\notin R.\n$$\n\n- We do not have enough information to determine whether $(2,2)$ is an element of $R.$\n\nLet's add this information to our table.\n\n|  | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $?$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f60a9114-56fc-5360-ae43-4601b4dee096" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From Adjacency Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "819b3f6f-52a3-5728-8062-d75fa6c027d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417462} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad93a7b-1be6-5fad-9a14-508b4f04a472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417469} {:answer/id #uuid "9df8d568-fa81-59d1-b253-8d8fd1d0a96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{?}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9df8d568-fa81-59d1-b253-8d8fd1d0a96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{?}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417478} :answer-field/id #uuid "115032cd-5992-5ff7-97ea-0d14f8ac773e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417551} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ebb2e93-8022-57a4-9309-f2a0a3c68666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417443} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea3d237-2d0b-5481-b345-cf516b83232b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{?}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417459} {:answer/id #uuid "b35cd459-3870-52ee-b096-503cece7131a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b35cd459-3870-52ee-b096-503cece7131a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417490} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3157c1-97ae-55f9-b99d-568c2329c41e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ac2da1-8554-5d1f-9e3f-187bd60e188f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417403} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecc13cf-1913-5af8-ac26-13b5f6a41492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417444} {:answer/id #uuid "78ef574b-3a2a-58e7-a1b7-3eb6093b5c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{?}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ecc13cf-1913-5af8-ac26-13b5f6a41492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417444} :answer-field/id #uuid "def00347-ec7a-5861-99e9-305b03e6f85f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d692895-cdfb-5939-b4c3-d559da7a492c" :question/math-academy-id "q-213498" :question/problem "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A={3,6,9}.$ Given that $R$ is antisymmetric, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix. \n\n**Note**: If there is not enough information to determine a particular cell's value, then select the question mark $(\\text{?}).$\n\n|  | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $1$ | $0$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $9$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ | $1$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWe are given elements of the adjacency matrix in the following highlighted cells outside the diagonal:\n\n|  | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ |  | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n| $9$ |  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nAs a result, the relation contains the pairs $(6,3)$ and $(9,6).$ Since $R$ must be antisymmetric, we require \n\n$$\n(3,6)\\notin R,\\,(6,9)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nSo, the cells corresponding to these elements must all be $0.$\n\n|  | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $9$ |  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nWe do not have enough information to determine whether $(9,3)$ is an element of $R.$ \n\n|  | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $9$ | $?$ | $1$ | $1$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cfbb87-25ee-57d3-9593-65b9056166c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417413} {:answer/id #uuid "681eaafc-b181-55c0-a5c1-50fc24ec41d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417438} {:answer/id #uuid "b65b6025-f874-5bd7-abde-81594d01d6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417491} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a402d1-6d3b-5a39-88ac-48a25a79c7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417509} {:answer/id #uuid "f66b2634-5d28-5976-9c01-6e76341a26f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b65b6025-f874-5bd7-abde-81594d01d6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417491} :answer-field/id #uuid "c65f5ba4-1fea-57cb-bbe2-73b81c041018" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee6fe66d-09a5-518d-9a32-a533c9b3cc75" :question/math-academy-id "q-213540" :question/problem "![](images/70/70743ac83b876995835703b35fc133af2133960ea916f589d60c8ac5636ee4ef.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph II represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26015440-1e7f-5a41-a515-cdb226ffde44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417401} {:answer/id #uuid "71e3b3fd-c2f3-5398-bd50-93961d57fa71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417447} {:answer/id #uuid "88da9920-b611-5173-8083-4d93b60bb921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417466} {:answer/id #uuid "d873a68d-5dbd-5f44-ba5e-d5e3260cbab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417518} {:answer/id #uuid "e701ebf7-eb6f-578b-a734-909bfb567074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71e3b3fd-c2f3-5398-bd50-93961d57fa71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417447} :answer-field/id #uuid "e73c30be-aa49-512c-9991-68a5517212a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd780cfc-a5dd-5c8f-a2c7-e44b9d2a3075" :question/math-academy-id "q-213543" :question/problem "![](images/da/dac9030db59b2b51b2b95c8bc9b5988106be8d198b7f947d33c8678d1cfa3b7b.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $1\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 1$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 3$ and $3\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107952e7-e54f-5bd6-a15c-561e0fb50805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "both symmetric and antisymmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417384} {:answer/id #uuid "28a979f6-f8e1-5a51-bc1f-5af3e30d62df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "neither symmetric nor antisymmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417404} {:answer/id #uuid "87ee0b9a-054c-5698-84e9-5b05ac9d9127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "antisymmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417465} {:answer/id #uuid "fc54d001-aa13-5198-b314-d9c36aa0262b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc54d001-aa13-5198-b314-d9c36aa0262b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417542} :answer-field/id #uuid "02053b66-2845-582e-8e76-34a823bf0145" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417549} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2189f23-7737-5c17-a2d1-60c5be1bf7a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417511} {:answer/id #uuid "e79a3dbb-ee98-5149-9be4-bf2562475770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417529} {:answer/id #uuid "fd82dec8-6ff9-55cb-8995-f705ffb16e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417545} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb13974-ca40-56fd-a077-50742f284147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdb13974-ca40-56fd-a077-50742f284147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417546} :answer-field/id #uuid "33210c90-7514-5606-9907-c6da63648dcc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417560} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25101369-af87-5340-a05b-1dc0d39ed92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417399} {:answer/id #uuid "6d39f2a0-5486-5378-81cb-b585a50d1c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417441} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0e5664-65e1-5b73-97ec-65306c7a3a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417485} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8109c0-d8c6-5ae0-bb31-ec32f5c3d51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d39f2a0-5486-5378-81cb-b585a50d1c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417441} :answer-field/id #uuid "79451f46-74c5-529d-979e-402bc8890e47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f3b0fa8-024e-5898-b1f6-7de09c79b487" :question/math-academy-id "q-213591" :question/problem "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $R$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0$ \n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⟹\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nThe relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, for any real numbers $x$ and $y,$ if ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ then ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=0.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nthe relation is $\\text{symmetric}.$\n\n**Note:** $R$ is not antisymmetric since, for example, ${1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=0$ and $(-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}=0$ but $1\\ne -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f80dfb-df35-526d-93aa-7c12d0a3e261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417421} {:answer/id #uuid "55898836-6dfe-51c6-83a5-9ff64545573d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,y≢x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417429} {:answer/id #uuid "75ef719b-d402-5575-a18f-8caaee79ba01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417450} {:answer/id #uuid "da5e3696-0ca1-503d-aa31-6d75dcee5517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≢y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46f80dfb-df35-526d-93aa-7c12d0a3e261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417421} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd6a87b7-410a-5366-be3c-03516297f6d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417582} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49153638-eff9-583d-9182-01f2b16ea66c" :answer/