 #uuid "7a9bb68b-cea4-5224-a811-49f66a6c4ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417456} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8807b95-a9a0-5be2-a1a0-c7bb474698e1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "452336ac-2206-5d26-872d-718b80ac9d74" :question/math-academy-id "q-213592" :question/problem "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $Z$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ \n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⟹\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nThe relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, for any integers $x$ and $y,$ if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2),$ then $y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,⟹\\,y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2),\n$$\n\nthe relation is $\\text{symmetric}.$\n\n**Note:** $R$ is not antisymmetric since, for example, $2≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ and $6≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ but $2\\ne 6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e66dab-c8a6-5999-a8d5-57978dab23a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "both symmetric and antisymmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417428} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1e6cc3-9b50-5e15-aff8-5c2533c354cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417470} {:answer/id #uuid "bb826659-a14b-5864-8aa5-c9d33c2d80c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "antisymmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417495} {:answer/id #uuid "c1371a86-dbcd-5e44-807d-683d47037294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "neither symmetric nor antisymmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb826659-a14b-5864-8aa5-c9d33c2d80c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "antisymmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417495} :answer-field/id #uuid "19f7c48d-1f9a-599b-ac9d-4656bdd17880" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417552} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "710a2ae9-b0fe-549d-bb91-b856a32d0bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417446} {:answer/id #uuid "7916f2b4-6729-5264-8dad-80fc5a938c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417453} {:answer/id #uuid "cd626b7a-5801-58e0-8fb4-e3aced40fbd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417508} {:answer/id #uuid "d79ac851-e3ae-5626-9ed3-cb22200381a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d79ac851-e3ae-5626-9ed3-cb22200381a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417517} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0285e7-c119-51cd-8805-066e7a4f897f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a69a6c5-e5bb-5333-b9a5-93cc8a4905a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le y\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\le x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417391} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe57770-3449-5f3e-8961-ce07a19a78c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le y\\,\\text{and}\\,y>x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417394} {:answer/id #uuid "50667484-86ab-505e-8426-40c287c49ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417427} {:answer/id #uuid "e45dcee2-f267-5b3e-82d6-18efebaf5f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a69a6c5-e5bb-5333-b9a5-93cc8a4905a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le y\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\le x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417391} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd507bb3-c245-55ad-8fbd-c90c0de96d16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ceafef3e-b20d-5064-9135-0687709767da" :question/math-academy-id "q-213594" :question/problem "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $R$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\le y$ \n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⟹\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nThe relation $R$ is antisymmetric. Indeed, for any real numbers $x$ and $y,$ if $x\\le y$ and $y\\le x,$ then $x$ and $y$ must be the same number.\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\nx\\le y\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\le x\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nthe relation is $\\text{antisymmetric}.$\n\n**Note:** $R$ is not symmetric since, for example, $0\\le 1$ but $1≰0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23dee3e5-8fb2-530f-bfa9-3599d563e0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417398} {:answer/id #uuid "2e24d934-08c4-58e3-be52-ce9f5121ebde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417408} {:answer/id #uuid "653569ac-ed4a-561b-b9be-9d858125d297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417436} {:answer/id #uuid "ead62325-324d-5dce-b93f-38dca6a0bf33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417531} {:answer/id #uuid "faab2d61-d391-5d78-81d3-04b266228963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$, $S,$ and $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faab2d61-d391-5d78-81d3-04b266228963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$, $S,$ and $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417541} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7a1b4f-e571-5ec0-a3c9-9dbfbbd624fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16c25cec-ba90-5814-9c8d-34f1bb30c2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-213599" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are symmetric? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x+y=0,$ defined on $R$\n\n- $X\\,T\\,Y$ if and only if $|X|=|Y|,$ defined on $P(N)$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ then $(y,x)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟹\\,y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in S$ then $(y,x)\\in S.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⟹\\,y+x=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in R.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y)\\in T$ then $(Y,X)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n|X|=|Y|\\,⟹\\,|Y|=|X|\n$$\n\nfor all $X,Y\\in P(N).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$, $S,$ and $T.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19eae037-9bb3-53cd-9405-bcdcf90085f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417390} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc5dc30-0af1-522a-98a7-9dc7cae28dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417393} {:answer/id #uuid "30083d0d-2315-5a99-8f56-85fda87401af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417409} {:answer/id #uuid "82cc221d-48ae-5487-9285-67993b828548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417464} {:answer/id #uuid "c5013afa-3560-51a7-8ce8-80f8c3522c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30083d0d-2315-5a99-8f56-85fda87401af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417409} :answer-field/id #uuid "b27f213d-f5cd-55a6-989e-1054aab40f4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe8a5755-a006-512f-a829-ac55db45a0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-213600" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are symmetric? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $y={x}^{2},$ defined on $R$\n\n- $X\\,S\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊂Y,$ defined on $P(R)$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $4∣(x+y),$ defined on $N$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(-1,1)\\in R$ since $1=(-1)^{2},$ while\n\n- $(1,-1)\\notin R$ since $-1\\ne {1}^{2}.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $({1},Z)\\in S$ since ${1}⊂Z,$ while\n\n- $(Z,{1})\\notin S$ since $Z⊄{1}.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in T$ then $(y,x)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n4∣(x+y)\\,⟹\\,4∣(y+x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\""}]}
